## Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project following the official Vercel documentation.

### Changes Made:

#### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (v2.0.1)
- Modified `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with dependency resolution

#### 2. Analytics Integration
- Modified `src/app/layout.js` to add the Analytics component
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next';`
- Added `<Analytics />` component at the end of the `<body>` tag in the RootLayout

### Implementation Details:

The implementation follows the Next.js App Router pattern as specified in the Vercel documentation:
- Analytics component is placed in the root layout to track all pages
- Component is positioned at the end of the body tag to ensure proper page tracking
- Uses the framework-specific import from `@vercel/analytics/next`

### Files Modified:
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new package and its dependencies
- `src/app/layout.js` - Added Analytics component import and usage

### Verification:
✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
✅ Linter ran successfully (no new errors introduced)
✅ All existing functionality preserved
✅ Lock file properly updated

### Next Steps for Production:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Project Settings → Analytics)
3. Verify analytics tracking by checking the Network tab for requests to `/_vercel/insights/*`
4. View analytics data in the Vercel dashboard after deployment

The Analytics component will automatically start tracking page views once the application is deployed to Vercel and analytics is enabled in the dashboard.
